<div class="lesson-body">
<header class="hero">
<div class="eyebrow">Statistics · Interactive lesson</div>
<h1>Binomial, Poisson and Exponential: Three Questions, Three Models</h1>
<p class="lead">Learn the distributions by asking the right question—not by memorizing formulas. Use Binomial for yes-or-no trials, Poisson for event counts, and Exponential for waiting time or distance.</p>
<div class="meta-row">
<span class="pill">Beginner</span><span class="pill">50 minutes</span><span class="pill">Interactive</span><span class="pill">Browser only</span>
</div>
<div class="why-matters"><strong>Why this matters:</strong> selecting the wrong distribution can produce the wrong probability, the wrong control chart, and the wrong operational decision—even when the arithmetic is flawless.</div>
<div class="big-memory">Binomial asks <strong>“How many selected outcomes out of n trials?”</strong> Poisson asks <strong>“How many events in an interval?”</strong> Exponential asks <strong>“How long until the next event?”</strong></div>
</header>
<section class="lesson-card" id="learning-objectives">
<h2>Learning objectives</h2>
<p>By the end of this lesson, you will be able to:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Identify</strong> whether a response is a binary-trial count, an event count, or a waiting-time measurement.</li>
<li><strong>Select</strong> Binomial, Poisson, or Exponential from the way the data are generated.</li>
<li><strong>Calculate</strong> exact, cumulative, tail, and interval probabilities.</li>
<li><strong>Explain</strong> how Poisson counts and Exponential waiting times arise from the same process.</li>
<li><strong>Choose</strong> between p, np, c, and u control-chart applications.</li>
<li><strong>Evaluate</strong> assumptions before trusting a probability or control-chart result.</li>
</ul>
<div class="audience-grid">
<div class="callout good"><strong>Prerequisite:</strong> No prior knowledge is required.</div>
<div class="callout"><strong>Target audience:</strong> quality professionals, engineers, analysts, students, and anyone who needs to model pass/fail outcomes, defect counts, arrivals, failures, or waiting times.</div>
<div class="callout"><strong>Tools:</strong> a modern browser. Excel and Minitab are optional for the software-guidance section.</div>
</div>
</section>
<section class="lesson-card" id="key-concepts">
<h2>1. Start with three different questions</h2>
<p>Imagine inspecting steel pipes. A pipe may pass or fail, defects may appear along its surface, and the distance between defects may vary. Those are three different random variables, so they require three different models.</p>
<div class="grid-3">
<div class="concept-card"><div class="icon" aria-hidden="true">✓ / ✕</div><div class="question">How many of 20 pipes fail inspection?</div><p class="answer-type">Answer: 0, 1, 2, …, 20</p><p><strong>Use Binomial.</strong> There is a fixed number of trials, and each pipe has two possible outcomes: fail or pass.</p></div>
<div class="concept-card"><div class="icon" aria-hidden="true">#</div><div class="question">How many defects occur in 100 metres?</div><p class="answer-type">Answer: 0, 1, 2, 3, …</p><p><strong>Use Poisson.</strong> You are counting random event occurrences inside a fixed interval.</p></div>
<div class="concept-card"><div class="icon" aria-hidden="true">↔</div><div class="question">How far until the next defect?</div><p class="answer-type">Answer: 4.2 m, 18.7 m, 53.1 m, …</p><p><strong>Use Exponential.</strong> You are measuring a continuous waiting distance between events.</p></div>
</div>
<div class="big-memory">Do not choose a distribution merely because the data are called a “count.” Choose it from the mechanism that generated the response.</div>
<div class="callout warn"><strong>What these models do not tell you:</strong> a probability model describes expected random variation under stated assumptions. It does not identify a root cause, prove independence, establish process stability, or guarantee that the rate or probability will remain unchanged.</div>
<h3>Key terminology</h3>
<div class="grid-3 terminology-grid">
<div class="term-card"><strong>Trial</strong><p><span class="term-label">Plain meaning:</span> one repeated opportunity.</p><p><span class="term-label">Technical meaning:</span> one Bernoulli experiment with two mutually exclusive outcomes.</p><p><span class="term-label">Practical interpretation:</span> one pipe, weld, order, or record that can be classified yes/no.</p></div>
<div class="term-card"><strong>Success probability, p</strong><p><span class="term-label">Plain meaning:</span> the chance of the selected outcome.</p><p><span class="term-label">Technical meaning:</span> the constant Bernoulli probability assigned to each Binomial trial.</p><p><span class="term-label">Practical interpretation:</span> “success” may mean failure, rejection, or any outcome you choose to count.</p></div>
<div class="term-card"><strong>Event</strong><p><span class="term-label">Plain meaning:</span> something that happens and is counted.</p><p><span class="term-label">Technical meaning:</span> an occurrence in a defined time, length, area, volume, or opportunity interval.</p><p><span class="term-label">Practical interpretation:</span> a defect, call, breakdown, inclusion, or arrival.</p></div>
<div class="term-card"><strong>Rate, λ</strong><p><span class="term-label">Plain meaning:</span> how frequently events occur.</p><p><span class="term-label">Technical meaning:</span> expected events per unit of exposure in a stationary Poisson process.</p><p><span class="term-label">Practical interpretation:</span> 2 defects per 100 m, 5 calls per hour, or 0.01 failures per day.</p></div>
<div class="term-card"><strong>Expected count, μ</strong><p><span class="term-label">Plain meaning:</span> the long-run average count in the chosen interval.</p><p><span class="term-label">Technical meaning:</span> μ = λt for a Poisson count over exposure t.</p><p><span class="term-label">Practical interpretation:</span> changing the inspection length changes μ even when λ stays constant.</p></div>
<div class="term-card"><strong>Gap or waiting time, T</strong><p><span class="term-label">Plain meaning:</span> the time or distance until the next event.</p><p><span class="term-label">Technical meaning:</span> a continuous random variable modelled by Exponential under a constant event rate.</p><p><span class="term-label">Practical interpretation:</span> metres until the next defect or hours until the next arrival.</p></div>
</div>
</section>
<!-- EDIT: Interactive model selector -->
<section class="lesson-card" id="model-selector">
<h2>2. Choose the model from the question</h2>
<p class="section-intro">Read how the response is generated, identify the data type, and select the model. “Neither” is available because a familiar distribution should not be forced onto a process that violates its assumptions.</p>
<div class="interactive-shell">
<div class="selector-scenario">
<h3 id="selectorTitle">Incoming inspection</h3>
<p id="selectorText">A supplier ships 40 coils. Each coil is classified as accepted or rejected. What models the number rejected?</p>
<div aria-label="Scenario clues" class="meta-row" id="selectorClues"></div>
</div>
<div aria-label="Choose a probability model" class="model-choice-grid">
<button class="model-answer" data-model-answer="binomial" type="button">Binomial</button>
<button class="model-answer" data-model-answer="poisson" type="button">Poisson</button>
<button class="model-answer" data-model-answer="exponential" type="button">Exponential</button>
<button class="model-answer" data-model-answer="neither" type="button">Neither / investigate</button>
</div>
<p aria-live="polite" class="status-line" id="selectorFeedback">Choose the model that matches how the response is generated.</p>
<div aria-label="Model selector score" class="kpi-strip">
<div class="kpi"><span>Correct</span><strong id="selectorCorrect">0</strong></div>
<div class="kpi"><span>Attempted</span><strong id="selectorAttempted">0</strong></div>
<div class="kpi"><span>Accuracy</span><strong id="selectorAccuracy">0%</strong></div>
</div>
<div class="button-row">
<button class="btn" id="nextScenarioBtn" type="button">Next scenario</button>
<button class="btn secondary" id="resetSelectorBtn" type="button">Reset trainer</button>
</div>
</div>
<div class="decision-lane">
<div class="decision-step"><strong>Fixed trials?</strong> A known number of units, each with yes/no outcomes, points to Binomial.</div>
<div class="decision-step"><strong>Events in exposure?</strong> Multiple events counted in a fixed time, length, area, or volume point to Poisson.</div>
<div class="decision-step"><strong>Gap to next event?</strong> A continuous waiting time or distance under a stable rate points to Exponential.</div>
</div>
</section>
